  <content>
  <section><emphasis><emphasis>
    <para>Students will complete the following questions to practice the skills they have learned in this lesson.</para>
  </emphasis></emphasis>
<para>Use the following to answer questions 1 – 7.</para>
<para>The table shows values of the expressions \(10x^2\) and \(2^x\).</para>
<table class="os-raise-skinnytable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry><emphasis>\(x\)</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>\(10x^2\)</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>\(2^x\)</emphasis></entry></row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>\(1\)</entry>
      <entry>\(10\)</entry>
      <entry>\(2\)</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>\(2\)</entry>
      <entry>\(40\)</entry>
      <entry>\(4\)</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>\(3\)</entry>
      <entry>\(90\)</entry>
      <entry>\(8\)</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>\(4\)</entry>
      <entry>\(160\)</entry>
      <entry>\(16\)</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>\(8\)</entry>
      <entry> </entry>
      <entry> </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>\(10\)</entry>
      <entry> </entry>
      <entry> </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>\(12\)</entry>
      <entry> </entry>
      <entry> </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>

<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>Complete the statements.</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
    <item> When the value of \(x\) increases by \(1\), the expression \(10x^2\):
      <list>
        <item> Grows by increasing amounts but by decreasing growth factors. </item>
        <item> Changes exponentially. It doubles. </item>
        <item> Grows by decreasing amounts but by increasing growth factors. </item>
        <item> Changes logarithmically. It has decreased by one-half. </item>
      </list>
    </item>
  </list>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Grows by increasing amounts but by decreasing growth factors.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
    <item> When the value of \(x\) increases by \(1\), the expression \(2^x\):
      <list>
        <item> Grows by increasing amounts but by decreasing growth factors. </item>
        <item> Changes exponentially. It doubles. </item>
        <item> Grows by decreasing amounts but by increasing growth factors. </item>
        <item> Changes logarithmically. It has decreased by one-half. </item>
      </list>
    </item>
  </list>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Changes exponentially. It doubles.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>Will the value of \(10x^2\) be greater than or less than \(2^x\) when \(x\) is \(8\)?
    <list>
      <item>greater than</item>
      <item>less than</item>
    </list>
  </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> greater than</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>Will the value of \(2^x\) be greater than or less than \(10x^2\) when \(x\) is \(10\)?
    <list>
      <item>greater than</item>
      <item>less than</item>
    </list>
  </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> greater than</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>Will the value of \(10x^2\) be greater than or less than \(2^x\) when \(x\) is \(12\)?
    <list>
      <item>greater than</item>
      <item>less than</item>
    </list>
  </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> less than</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item>Determine the following values of \(10x^2\).</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
    <item> What is the value of \(10x^2\) when \(x\) is \(8\)? </item>
  </list>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(640\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
    <item> What is the value of \(10x^2\) when \(x\) is \(10\)? </item>
  </list>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(1000\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
    <item> What is the value of \(10x^2\) when \(x\) is \(12\)?</item>
  </list>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(1440\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="6" type="enumerated">
  <item>Determine the following values of \(2^x\).</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
    <item> What is the value of \(2^x\) when \(x\) is \(8\)? </item>
  </list>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(256\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
    <item> What is the value of \(2^x\) when \(x\) is \(10\)? </item>
  </list>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(1024\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
    <item> What is the value of \(2^x\) when \(x\) is \(12\)? </item>
  </list>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(4096\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="7" type="enumerated">
  <item>Choose the best statement that describes how the values of the exponential expression \(2^x\) and the quadratic
    expression \(10x^2\) change as \(x\) becomes greater and greater.
</item></list>
<list>
  <item> The value of the exponential expression grows much more quickly as \(x\) increases. </item>
  <item> The value of the quadratic expression grows much more quickly as \(x\) increases. </item>
  <item> They both grow at the same rate. </item>
  <item> There is no change in the values of the expressions when \(x\) increases. </item>
</list>


<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> The value of the exponential expression grows much more quickly as \(x\) increases.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="8" type="enumerated">
  <item>Answer the following questions about functions \(f(x)\) and \(g(x)\).</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
    <item> What type of expression is \(f(x)=1.5^x\)?
      <list>
        <item>exponential</item>
        <item>quadratic</item>
        <item>linear</item>
      </list>
    </item>
  </list>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> exponential</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
    <item> What type of expression is \(g(x)=500x^2+345x\)?
      <list>
        <item>exponential</item>
        <item>quadratic</item>
        <item>linear</item>
      </list>
    </item>
  </list>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> quadratic</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
    <item> The values of which function will eventually be greater for larger and larger values of \(x\)?
      <list>
        <item>Function \(f(x)\)</item>
        <item>Function \(g(x)\)</item>
      </list>
    </item>
  </list>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Function \(f(x)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="9" type="enumerated">
  <item>The table shows the values of \(4^x\) and \(100x^2\) for some values of \(x\).</item>
</list>
<table class="os-raise-skinnytable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry><emphasis>\(x\)</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>\(4^x\)</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>\(100x^2\)</emphasis></entry></row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>\(1\)</entry>
      <entry>\(4\)</entry>
      <entry>\(100\)</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>\(2\)</entry>
      <entry>\(16\)</entry>
      <entry>\(400\)</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>\(3\)</entry>
      <entry>\(64\)</entry>
      <entry>\(900\)</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>\(4\)</entry>
      <entry>\(256\)</entry>
      <entry>\(1,600\)</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>\(5\)</entry>
      <entry>\(1,024\)</entry>
      <entry>\(2,500\)</entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>

<para>Looking at the patterns in the table, choose the best answer to explain why, as the value of \(x\) increases, the
  values of the exponential expression \(4^x\) will eventually overtake the values of the quadratic expression
  \(100x^2\).</para>

<list>
  <item>The value of \(100x^2\) grows by different factors, and the value of \(4^x\) grows by a factor of \(4\) every
    time. </item>
  <item> The value of \(100x^2\) grows by a factor of \(4\) every time, and the value of \(4^x\) grows by different
    factors. </item>
  <item> The value of \(100x^2\) grows by different factors, and the value of \(4^x\) grows by a factor of \(x\) every
    time. </item>
  <item> The value of \(100x^2\) grows by a factor of \(x\) every time, and the value of \(4^x\) grows by different
    factors.</item>
</list>

<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> The value of \(100x^2\) grows by different factors, and the value of \(4^x\) grows by a
  factor of \(4\) every time.</para></section>
